  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows little movement in the expansive ridge over \nthe eastern Rockies. Same goes for the midlevel ridge across the \nGulf coast. Surface winds out of the southwest boosted by enhanced \nlow level flow into the region maintains a steady stream of warm air \nadvection. Shortwave troughs aloft have been consistently pummeling \nthe ridge slowly redirecting upper level flow to more northerly. \nThis has been advecting drier air into the area which has reduced \nour dew points from near 80 to the upper 70s. Ironically, this might \nincrease our chances of reaching triple digits today as less energy \nis required to heat up drier air compared to saturated air. Highs \ntoday look more likely to reach triple digits with HREF ensembles \nshowing 70-90 percent chances of patches of 100F air temperatures \nalong and west of the I-29/I-49 corridors. This also results in a \nzero sum of heat index values with the increase in temperatures \nnegating the decrease in dew points. Heat index values around 110F-\n115F with areas of 120F expected. Overnight lows once again reside \nin the upper 70s Monday into Tuesday.\n\nAs mentioned previously, passing shortwave troughs have been \ngradually weakening the ridge's hold on the area. Over time, this \ncreates more exposure to pockets of cyclonic vorticity advection \nwhich brings the potential for isolated shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment. Guidance was proposing the first opportunity for this \nearly this morning; however, observations have been less favorable \nfor anything more than an isolated shower occurring. The next \nsubstantial opportunity is late this afternoon as a more significant \nshortwave traverses IL with the boundary extending as far west as \nKC. Deterministic guidance is a little more confident in showers \ncoupling the previously mentioned trough across the Great Lakes. CAM \nguidance is a little less optimistic about storm development, but \ndoes paint the potential for some storms to spread as far west as \nHiggensville. This may work to quickly lower heat indices later this \nafternoon across central MO, but confidence remains fairly low at \nthis time. The good news is this passing trough does introduce some \n\"cooler air\" into the region. Highs Tuesday reside in the low 90s \nwith heat indices looking to top out around 100F sans the far south \nof the region where heat index values up to 105F are possible. \n\nLooking further, the established upper level high remains planted \nacross the Rocky Mountains which keeps the upper level relatively \nunchanged. However, shortwaves continue to transit the upper level \nflow, and the eastward expanse of the upper level ridge loses a \nlittle influence. Ridging at lower level moves eastward and opens up \nthe potential for some more turbulent dynamics. Extended guidance \nmanifests this a potential leeward midlevel troughs influenced by \nthe upper level shortwaves mentioned earlier. This presents multiple \nopportunities for precipitation later in the forecast period, but it \nwould be another example of everything coming together in the right \nplace at the right time which adds some uncertainty. Consensus puts \nmost of these precipitation opportunities during the overnight \nperiods.\n\nOverall, temperatures trend lower as drier and relatively cooler air \nflows into the region. Highs late week look to return into the upper \n80s. Passing overnight convection may work to accelerate that \ncooling trend; although residual low level moisture from storms may \nnot make it feel much cooler. Extended guidance does show the \npotential for a wholesale change in temperatures (at least for a \nlittle bit) next weekend as a synoptic scale front is progged to \nmigrate through the central CONUS.",
